---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioCompressGif from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioCompressGif.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="resize-gif">
  <GifAudioCompressGif client:load mode="resize" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to resize an animated GIF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the GIF in. The tool reads its dimensions, frame count, frame rate and loop setting from the file header before it decodes anything.</li>
      <li>Pick a preset or type a width. The height follows; change it only if you want to distort the image.</li>
      <li>Leave the palette at 256 colours unless you also have a file-size limit.</li>
      <li>Press resize, compare the result with the original above it, and download.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Common GIF sizes</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Width</th><th>Used for</th><th>Also constrained by</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>128 px</td><td>Discord custom emoji and stickers</td><td>256 KB file size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>128 px</td><td>Slack custom emoji</td><td>128 KB file size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>256 px</td><td>Forum avatars, small reaction images</td><td>Often 512 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>320 px</td><td>Inline chat images, mobile-first content</td><td>No other limit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>480 px</td><td>README files, documentation, changelogs</td><td>GitHub renders at container width</td></tr>
          <tr><td>640 px</td><td>Full-width blog posts on a phone screen</td><td>Page-weight budgets</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why simple GIF resizing looks bad</h2>
    <p>
      A GIF is already quantised: each frame is an index into a table of at most 256 colours. When you
      scale it, the resampling filter averages neighbouring pixels, and those averages are new colours
      that are not in the table. A simple resizer then moves each new pixel to the nearest entry in the
      original palette. If that palette was built for large flat areas, the intermediate tones from scaling
      have no good match, and you get blotches and colour drift.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool runs FFmpeg’s two-pass palette pipeline after scaling. It analyses the scaled frames to
      build a new 256-colour palette from the colours the resized animation contains, then maps the frames
      onto it with dithering. The extra pass costs a second or two, and it makes a 128 px emoji clear in
      place of blotchy.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the Lanczos filter is used</h2>
    <p>
      Scaling uses the Lanczos filter, a windowed sinc kernel that samples a wider neighbourhood than
      bilinear or bicubic interpolation. It keeps edges and fine text sharper when you shrink by large
      factors, as from a 500 px screen recording to a 128 px emoji. The cost is slight ringing around very
      high-contrast edges, which is invisible at these sizes.
    </p>

    <h2>What is kept and what changes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Kept: frame count, each per-frame delay, the Netscape loop block (an infinite GIF stays infinite) and playback speed.</li>
      <li>Changed: pixel dimensions, the colour palette, and so the file size.</li>
      <li>Removed: comment and application extension blocks other than the loop count. Authoring tools often put identifying metadata there.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Can I enlarge a GIF?</h2>
    <p>
      You can type a larger width, and Lanczos does a reasonable job. But the source has only 256 colours,
      and no filter recovers detail that was never stored. Enlarging also multiplies the file size by the
      area ratio: double the width and the bytes roughly quadruple. If the animation came from a video,
      export it again from the original at the size you need with the
      <a href="/video-to-gif">video to GIF</a> tool.
    </p>

    <h2>Resize first, then compress</h2>
    <p>
      Resolution has the largest effect on GIF size, so resize first; often that is enough. If the resized
      file is still over a hard byte limit, such as a 128 KB Slack emoji, use
      <a href="/compress-gif">Compress GIF</a>. It searches frame rate and palette settings for the best
      version that fits. If you compress first, the compressor works on pixels you then discard.
    </p>

    <h2>File size and offline use</h2>
    <p>
      FFmpeg, compiled to WebAssembly, decodes and re-encodes the GIF inside this tab. There is no daily
      limit, no watermark and no maximum file size other than your device’s memory. After the engine is
      cached, the page works with the network off.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
